# Modern India and the national movement, 1757 to 1947.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    L(1, 2, "Land revenue", stmts(CS, [
        "The Permanent Settlement of 1793 fixed the land revenue demand on zamindars for all time.",
        "The Ryotwari system was introduced mainly in Bengal and Bihar.",
    ]), TWO, 0,
      "Only statement 1 is correct. Cornwallis's Permanent Settlement fixed the revenue owed by zamindars in perpetuity. The Ryotwari system, settled directly with cultivators, was introduced mainly in the Madras and Bombay Presidencies."),
    S(1, 2, "Social reform", "The Atmiya Sabha, set up in Calcutta in 1815, was founded by:",
      ["Raja Rammohan Roy", "Debendranath Tagore", "Keshab Chandra Sen", "Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar"],
      "Rammohan Roy founded the Atmiya Sabha in 1815 to discuss monotheism and social reform, and the Brahmo Sabha (later Brahmo Samaj) in 1828. Debendranath Tagore and Keshab Chandra Sen led the Brahmo movement later."),
    L(1, 2, "Early Congress", stmts("Which of the following were among the demands of the early (Moderate) leaders of the Indian National Congress?", [
        "Expansion of the legislative councils with more elected Indian members",
        "Holding the Indian Civil Service examination in India as well as in England",
        "Complete independence from British rule",
    ], "Select the correct answer using the code given below."), THREE, 0,
      "The Moderates asked for reform within British rule: larger councils with elected members, simultaneous ICS examinations, cuts in military spending and the like. Complete independence (Purna Swaraj) was adopted as the goal only at the Lahore session of 1929."),
    L(1, 2, "Press laws", stmts("Consider the following statements about the Vernacular Press Act, 1878:", [
        "It did not apply to newspapers published in English.",
        "It was repealed during the viceroyalty of Lord Ripon.",
    ]), TWO, 2,
      "Both are correct. The Act, passed under Lord Lytton, targeted newspapers in Indian languages only, which is why it was called the 'gagging Act'. Lord Ripon's government repealed it in 1882."),
    L(1, 2, "Constitutional development", stmts("Consider the following statements about the Government of India Act, 1935:", [
        "It provided for an All-India Federation of provinces and princely states.",
        "It introduced provincial autonomy.",
        "It ended dyarchy in the provinces.",
    ], HOW_ASK), HOW_MANY, 2,
      "All three are correct. The Act proposed a federation (which never came into being because the princes did not join), gave the provinces autonomy with responsible ministries, and replaced provincial dyarchy while introducing dyarchy at the centre."),
    S(1, 1, "Economic critique", "The book Poverty and Un-British Rule in India, which set out the drain of wealth theory, was written by:",
      ["Dadabhai Naoroji", "R. C. Dutt", "M. G. Ranade", "G. K. Gokhale"],
      "Dadabhai Naoroji published Poverty and Un-British Rule in India in 1901. R. C. Dutt also wrote on the drain in his Economic History of India."),
    L(1, 2, "Gandhian movements", stmts(CS, [
        "The Champaran Satyagraha of 1917 was a struggle over the wages of cotton mill workers.",
        "The Kheda Satyagraha of 1918 was against the tinkathia system of indigo cultivation.",
    ]), TWO, 3,
      "Neither is correct. Champaran (1917) was against the tinkathia system that forced peasants to grow indigo on part of their land. Kheda (1918) sought remission of land revenue after crop failure. The 1918 dispute over mill workers' wages was in Ahmedabad."),
    S(1, 2, "Congress sessions", "The resolution on Fundamental Rights and Economic Policy was adopted at which session of the Indian National Congress?",
      ["Karachi, 1931", "Lahore, 1929", "Lucknow, 1916", "Haripura, 1938"],
      "The Karachi session of 1931, presided over by Vallabhbhai Patel, adopted the resolution on Fundamental Rights and Economic Policy. Lahore 1929 adopted Purna Swaraj and Lucknow 1916 saw the pact with the Muslim League."),
    L(1, 3, "Constitutional development", stmts("Which of the following were features of the Indian Councils Act, 1909?", [
        "Separate electorates for Muslims",
        "Dyarchy in the provinces",
        "Indirect election of members to the legislative councils",
    ], "Select the correct answer using the code given below."), THREE_B, 2,
      "The Morley-Minto Reforms of 1909 introduced separate electorates for Muslims and elected members chosen indirectly through local bodies and other electorates. Dyarchy in the provinces came with the Act of 1919."),
    S(1, 2, "Depressed classes", "The Poona Pact of 1932 between Gandhi and B. R. Ambedkar provided for:",
      ["Reserved seats within the general electorate", "Separate electorates for the Depressed Classes", "Abolition of untouchability by law", "Reserved posts for the Depressed Classes in the ICS"],
      "The Communal Award had given the Depressed Classes separate electorates. Gandhi fasted against it, and the Poona Pact replaced them with a larger number of reserved seats within the general electorate."),
    S(1, 2, "Civil Disobedience", "Who led the salt march from Trichinopoly to Vedaranyam on the Tamil coast in 1930?",
      ["C. Rajagopalachari", "K. Kamaraj", "E. V. Ramasamy", "S. Satyamurti"],
      "C. Rajagopalachari led the Vedaranyam salt march in April 1930, the Tamil counterpart of Gandhi's Dandi march."),
    S(1, 2, "Towards independence", "The Cabinet Mission Plan of 1946 proposed:",
      ["A Union with provinces grouped into sections", "Immediate partition into two sovereign dominions", "Dominion status with separate electorates for all", "Direct British rule until a war crimes inquiry"],
      "The Cabinet Mission rejected a sovereign Pakistan and proposed a Union handling defence, foreign affairs and communications, with provinces grouped into three sections and a Constituent Assembly elected by the provincial assemblies."),
    L(1, 2, "Press and nationalism", stmts(CP, [
        "Kesari : Bal Gangadhar Tilak",
        "Al-Hilal : Maulana Abul Kalam Azad",
        "The Bengalee : Aurobindo Ghose",
    ], PAIRS_ASK), HOW_MANY_PAIRS, 1,
      "Two pairs are correct. Tilak ran the Marathi Kesari and Azad founded the Urdu weekly Al-Hilal in 1912. The Bengalee was edited by Surendranath Banerjea; Aurobindo is linked with Bande Mataram and Karmayogin."),
    L(1, 2, "Racial controversy", stmts("Consider the following statements about the Ilbert Bill of 1883:", [
        "It was introduced during the viceroyalty of Lord Ripon.",
        "It was passed in its original form despite European protests.",
    ]), TWO, 0,
      "Only statement 1 is correct. The Bill would have let Indian judges try Europeans in district courts. European protest forced a compromise under which a European could demand a jury of which half were Europeans."),

    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "British expansion", "The Subsidiary Alliance system, under which Indian rulers paid for British troops stationed in their states, is associated with:",
      ["Lord Wellesley", "Lord Cornwallis", "Lord Dalhousie", "Warren Hastings"],
      "Lord Wellesley (1798 to 1805) used the Subsidiary Alliance to bring Hyderabad, Awadh and other states under British control. Dalhousie is known for the Doctrine of Lapse."),
    L(2, 2, "Company rule", stmts(CS, [
        "The Regulating Act of 1773 created the office of Governor-General of Bengal.",
        "The Charter Act of 1833 ended the East India Company's activities as a trading body.",
    ]), TWO, 2,
      "Both are correct. The Regulating Act made Warren Hastings the first Governor-General of Bengal, and the Charter Act of 1833 ended the Company's remaining trade, including with China, leaving it an administrative body. It also created the post of Governor-General of India."),
    L(2, 2, "Revolt of 1857", stmts(CP, [
        "Khan Bahadur Khan : Bareilly",
        "Kunwar Singh : Jhansi",
        "Begum Hazrat Mahal : Lucknow",
    ], PAIRS_ASK), HOW_MANY_PAIRS, 1,
      "Two pairs are correct. Khan Bahadur Khan led at Bareilly and Begum Hazrat Mahal at Lucknow. Kunwar Singh led the revolt at Jagdishpur in Bihar; Jhansi was led by Rani Lakshmibai."),
    L(2, 2, "Social reform", stmts(CS, [
        "The Satyashodhak Samaj was founded by Jyotirao Phule.",
        "The Self-Respect Movement was started by E. V. Ramasamy.",
        "The Prarthana Samaj was founded in Calcutta.",
    ], HOW_ASK), HOW_MANY, 1,
      "Two are correct. Phule founded the Satyashodhak Samaj in 1873 and Periyar started the Self-Respect Movement in 1925. The Prarthana Samaj was founded in Bombay in 1867."),
    S(2, 2, "Swadeshi", "Which of the following was part of the Swadeshi movement that followed the partition of Bengal?",
      ["Setting up national schools such as the Bengal National College", "Non-payment of land revenue across British India", "The salt march to the sea at Dandi", "The demand for complete independence at Lahore"],
      "Swadeshi (1905 to 1911) promoted Indian goods, boycott of British cloth and national education, including the National Council of Education and the Bengal National College. Dandi (1930) and Purna Swaraj at Lahore (1929) came much later."),
    L(2, 3, "Lucknow, 1916", stmts("Which of the following happened at or through the Lucknow session of the Indian National Congress in 1916?", [
        "The Congress accepted separate electorates for Muslims in a pact with the Muslim League.",
        "The Moderates and Extremists were reunited in the Congress.",
        "The Congress and the League put forward joint demands for constitutional reform.",
    ], "Select the correct answer using the code given below."), THREE, 3,
      "All three. The Lucknow session reunited the two wings that had split at Surat in 1907, and the Lucknow Pact with the Muslim League accepted separate electorates and set out joint demands for self-government."),
    S(2, 2, "INA", "The Provisional Government of Free India (Azad Hind) was proclaimed in 1943 at:",
      ["Singapore", "Rangoon", "Tokyo", "Berlin"],
      "Subhas Chandra Bose proclaimed the Provisional Government of Azad Hind at Singapore in October 1943. Its headquarters later moved to Rangoon."),
    L(2, 2, "Rowlatt and Jallianwala", stmts(CS, [
        "The Rowlatt Act of 1919 allowed political suspects to be detained without trial.",
        "The Hunter Committee that inquired into the Jallianwala Bagh massacre was appointed by the Indian National Congress.",
    ]), TWO, 0,
      "Only statement 1 is correct. The Hunter Committee was appointed by the Government of India. The Congress set up its own inquiry committee, whose report was more critical of General Dyer."),
    L(2, 2, "Non-Cooperation", stmts("Which of the following were part of the programme of the Non-Cooperation Movement adopted in 1920?", [
        "Surrender of titles and honorary offices",
        "Boycott of government schools, colleges and law courts",
        "Boycott of the elections to the reformed councils",
    ], "Select the correct answer using the code given below."), THREE, 3,
      "All three. The Calcutta special session and the Nagpur session of 1920 adopted surrender of titles, boycott of schools, courts and the 1920 council elections, and the boycott of foreign cloth, with civil disobedience and non-payment of taxes held back for a later stage."),
    L(2, 2, "Peasant and tribal movements", stmts(CP, [
        "Santhal Hul of 1855 : Rajmahal hills",
        "Pabna agrarian league : Bengal",
        "Eka movement : Punjab",
    ], PAIRS_ASK), HOW_MANY_PAIRS, 1,
      "Two pairs are correct. The Santhal rising was in the Rajmahal hills region and the Pabna leagues of the 1870s in eastern Bengal. The Eka (unity) movement of 1921 to 1922 was among peasants of Awadh in the United Provinces."),
    S(2, 2, "Constitutional development", "The Government of India Act, 1919 introduced:",
      ["Dyarchy in the provinces", "Provincial autonomy", "A Federal Court", "Separate electorates for Muslims"],
      "The Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms of 1919 divided provincial subjects into reserved and transferred lists, which is dyarchy. Provincial autonomy and a Federal Court came with the 1935 Act, and Muslim separate electorates with the 1909 Act."),
    S(2, 2, "Communal Award", "The Communal Award of 1932 was announced by the British Prime Minister:",
      ["Ramsay MacDonald", "Winston Churchill", "Clement Attlee", "Stanley Baldwin"],
      "Ramsay MacDonald announced the Communal Award in August 1932, extending separate electorates, including to the Depressed Classes. Attlee was Prime Minister at independence."),
    L(2, 2, "Quit India", stmts(CS, [
        "The Quit India Movement was launched after the failure of the Cripps Mission.",
        "A parallel government (prati sarkar) was set up in Satara during the movement.",
    ]), TWO, 2,
      "Both are correct. The Cripps Mission of March and April 1942 offered only dominion status after the war, and its failure led the AICC to adopt the Quit India resolution in August 1942. Parallel governments arose in Satara, Ballia and Tamluk."),
    S(2, 2, "Round Table Conferences", "Which of the following is true of the First Round Table Conference, held in London in 1930 and 1931?",
      ["The Indian National Congress did not take part", "Gandhi attended it as the Congress's sole delegate", "It ended with the signing of the Poona Pact", "It was held in Delhi with the princes"],
      "The Congress boycotted the First Round Table Conference because Civil Disobedience was under way. After the Gandhi-Irwin Pact, Gandhi attended the Second Conference in 1931 as the sole Congress representative."),

    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 1, "Social reform", "Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar campaigned successfully for which law?",
      ["The Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act, 1856", "The Bengal Sati Regulation, 1829", "The Age of Consent Act, 1891", "The Child Marriage Restraint Act, 1929"],
      "Vidyasagar's campaign led to the Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act of 1856. The ban on sati in 1829 owed much to Rammohan Roy."),
    L(0, 2, "Independence", stmts("Consider the following statements about the Indian Independence Act, 1947:", [
        "It ended the paramountcy of the British Crown over the princely states.",
        "Under it, the Constituent Assembly also served as the legislature of the Dominion of India.",
    ]), TWO, 2,
      "Both are correct. Paramountcy lapsed on 15 August 1947, leaving the princely states to accede, and the Constituent Assembly worked as the dominion legislature until the Constitution came into force."),
]
